No it doesn't.  That may be a common inference, but "interrupt" is not 
defined as "cause".  Your argument might hold for sisti rinka, because it 
is a rinka, or maybe even sistyrinka if dikyjvo conventions apply.

>X is the cause, Z the mechanism.
>Whatever Z is, it also involves Y, obviously: if the mechanism of
>interruption had absolutely nothing to do with Y, then why was Y
>interrupted in the first place? And the slots for X and Y in Z are filled
>with ce'u. To say those slots are unfillable is to say that there is no 
>causal connection between X and Z, or Y and Z. Which is absurd.

I guess I am in favor of allowing absurdity.

